<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iraq</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iraq is a region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> rivers, the land of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Its rich agricultural lands made it a prize for many empires, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, the Umayyad and Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. In the early modern period it was contested between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iraq.jpg" alt="Iraq" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iraq.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zanj Rebellion (869–883), a massive revolt of enslaved labourers in southern <b>Iraq</b>, devastated the agricultural heartland and contributed materially to the caliphate's later economic decline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Perhaps 100,000 Arabs then lived in Ifriqiya, most of them immigrants from Syria and <b>Iraq</b>, but the Berbers remained the great majority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>These victories made Uzun Hasan ruler of Azerbaijan, <b>Iraq</b> and much of Iran.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Syria-Palestine corridor was one of the most commercially vital regions of the medieval world, connecting the overland routes from <b>Iraq</b>, Persia and Central Asia to the Mediterranean ports of Acre, Beirut and Tyre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By 2021 the armed forces had 126,000 active personnel and 17,000 paramilitary troops, a defence budget of $2.24 billion in 2020, 5.4 percent of GDP, and an arms industry of its own; Azerbaijan cooperates with NATO through the Partnership for Peace and sent peacekeepers to <b>Iraq</b> and Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of the population was Hindu, while the ruling class included Deccani Muslims, descended from earlier settlers from northern India, and newcomers from Iran, <b>Iraq</b>, Central Asia and Arabia, known as Afaqis or &quot;westerners&quot;, as well as Abyssinians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
